{"author":{"id":"ad87ffa09351c4e5a412ec8ee1546e86b314f33d6f28629bb4bcfe7b2290924d"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794303320,"nonce":"93cRpIoaWuWGa2KL2OOZYn9UqK8=","title":"GET /api/documents lists every document at once: paginate it","message":"GET /api/documents (Explore, listDocuments in reader/src/lib/api.ts) returns every public entry plus the viewer's private ones in one response: DOCUMENT_QUERIES.list in api/src/worker/adapters.ts has no LIMIT, and Explore renders them all. GET /api/me/documents (importedBy) is the same for a user's own.\n\nIt costs more than the rows: documents.list (api/src/controllers/documents.ts) calls current() for each one, which reads its status.json from R2, and requeues it if it's stuck or its analysis is outdated. So one listing is a read from R2 per document, and after a lexicon or analyzer version bump it can mean a write and a queue send per document. Check whether those binding calls count toward the Worker's per-request subrequest limit: if they do, the list fails outright past that many documents.\n\nTo do:\n- Keyset pagination, not OFFSET: order by (imported_at, document_id, user_id) and pass the last row as a cursor (?after=…\u0026limit=…, a default and a maximum), returning the next cursor. The public/private UNION needs the cursor in both halves, with an index to match.\n- Explore loads more as you scroll (or a More button), and keeps its filters working across pages (they're client-side today).\n- Move the stuck and outdated requeue out of listing: a scheduled sweep, or only when a document is opened (documents.get), so a page of the list reads only D1 and its documents' statuses, or the status is kept in D1.","files":null}]}